node_modules/
.next/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
data/source/*
!data/source/README.md
*.log
.DS_Store

# Next.js auto-generated; re-created on every build or dev run
next-env.d.ts

# Local Supabase CA bundle for scripts/dev-db-sql.mjs (public cert, but machine-local)
*.crt

# Crawler: per-run state (checksums, timestamps) — tracked now, not ignored (see the file's
# own history/the crawler redesign notes): the GitHub Actions cache that's meant to persist
# this across runs only exists once a run has actually saved one, so the FIRST real run after
# any cache change would still see zero state and silently re-detect (incorrectly, for several
# books) every hand-verified textbook boundary. Committing a correct baseline means every fresh
# checkout — CI included — starts from known-good state regardless of cache history. No
# secrets/PII in this file (just checksums, timestamps, status strings), so committing it is
# safe. Update it (or let a real crawl run update it) whenever a manifest entry's status
# changes for real, the same way a lockfile gets updated.

# Crawler: cached OCR output per PDF checksum — OCR is the slowest step (20-30+ min for a full
# textbook) and deterministic for an unchanged file, so re-running it after an ingestion-only
# code change (e.g. tuning chapter detection) is pure waste. Machine-local, not for VCS — can
# get large and is trivially regenerable.
data/.ocr-cache/
# Crawler redesign, Phase 1: cached downloaded PDF bytes, keyed by URL — some source hosts
# (taleem360) intermittently rate-limit a URL just downloaded successfully moments earlier,
# and re-fetching a 40-70MB textbook just to re-run detection logic against unchanged bytes
# is pure waste, same reasoning as the OCR cache above. Machine-local, not for VCS.
data/.pdf-cache/

# Tesseract language data — downloaded at runtime by tesseract.js, not for VCS
*.traineddata
